<$fill $name="body">

!! Q: Why does my number show up as a string in the JSON output?

All values default to `type="string"`. To get a numeric value, set `type="number"` explicitly on `<$json-prop>`, `<$json-item>`, or `<$json-val>`. Example: `<$json-prop key="count" value="42" type="number"/>`.

!! Q: The JSON tree is rebuilt on every refresh — will this be slow for large structures?

JSON DSL widgets use `refreshSelf` — the entire tree is rebuilt on any change to keep logic stateless. For most use cases this is fast enough. If you have a very large structure (hundreds of properties), consider using `<$json-save>` to persist the result to a data tiddler and only rebuild when inputs change.

!! Q: Can I use JSON DSL on the server side?

No. JSON DSL widgets are browser-side only — they build the JSON tree during the TiddlyWiki render cycle and output it as DOM text nodes or via `wiki.setText()`. Server-side rendering does not execute widget `execute()` in the same way.

